{"tool_name":"tabix","contents":["biocontainers","oeb","bioschemas","biotools"],"fetched_metadata":{"biocontainers":{"name":"tabix","identifiers":["doi:10.1093/bioinformatics/btq671"],"license":"MIT","summary":"Library for reading/writing high-throughput sequencing data. is utilized and incorporated into both samtools and bcftools.","home_url":"https://github.com/samtools/htslib","keywords":["Computational Biology","Genome","Genomics","Information Storage and Retrieval","Sequence Analysis, DNA"],"manually_check":true,"total_pulls":45860},"biotools":{"id":"tabix","home":"http://www.htslib.org/doc/tabix.html","summary":"Tabix indexes a TAB-delimited genome position file in.tab.bgz and creates an index file (in.tab.bgz.tbi or in.tab.bgz.csi) when region is absent from the command-line. The input data file must be position sorted and compressed by bgzip which has a gzip(1) like interface.\n\nAfter indexing, tabix is able to quickly retrieve data lines overlapping regions specified in the format \"chr:beginPos-endPos\". (Coordinates specified in this region format are 1-based and inclusive.)\n\nFast data retrieval also works over network if URI is given as a file name and in this case the index file will be downloaded if it is not present locally.\n\nThe tabix (.tbi) and BAI index formats can handle individual chromosomes up to 512 Mbp (2^29 bases) in length. If your input file might contain data lines with begin or end positions greater than that, you will need to use a CSI index.","addition_date":"2021-04-22T01:06:12Z","last_update_date":"2023-01-11T01:11:56.773187Z","tool_type":["Command-line tool"],"accessibility":"Open access","biotools_curie":"biotools:tabix","cost":"Free of charge","credit":[{"name":"Heng Li"}],"edit_permission":{"authors":["alexcorm"],"type":"group"},"function":[{"input":[{"data":{"term":"URI","uri":"http://edamontology.org/data_1047"},"format":[{"term":"BAI","uri":"http://edamontology.org/format_3327"},{"term":"TSV","uri":"http://edamontology.org/format_3475"},{"term":"bgzip","uri":"http://edamontology.org/format_3615"},{"term":"tabix","uri":"http://edamontology.org/format_3616"}]}],"operation":[{"term":"Data handling","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_2409"},{"term":"Data retrieval","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_2422"},{"term":"Sorting","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_3802"}]}],"name":"tabix","operating_system":["Linux","Mac","Windows"],"owner":"leipzig","topic":[{"term":"Genomics","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_0622"}]},"bioschemas":{"name":"tabix","home":"https://bio.tools/tabix","summary":"Tabix indexes a TAB-delimited genome position file in.tab.bgz and creates an index file (in.tab.bgz.tbi or in.tab.bgz.csi) when region is absent from the command-line. The input data file must be position sorted and compressed by bgzip which has a gzip(1) like interface.\n\nAfter indexing, tabix is able to quickly retrieve data lines overlapping regions specified in the format \"chr:beginPos-endPos\". (Coordinates specified in this region format are 1-based and inclusive.)\n\nFast data retrieval also works over network if URI is given as a file name and in this case the index file will be downloaded if it is not present locally.\n\nThe tabix (.tbi) and BAI index formats can handle individual chromosomes up to 512 Mbp (2^29 bases) in length. If your input file might contain data lines with begin or end positions greater than that, you will need to use a CSI index.","tool_type":"sc:SoftwareApplication"},"oeb":{"metrics":[{"@id":"https://openebench.bsc.es/monitor/metrics/biotools:tabix/cmd/www.htslib.org","@license":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/","@nmsp":"biotools","@type":"metrics","deprecated":true,"project":{"build":{},"deployment":{},"publications":[],"summary":{"description":true},"website":{"bioschemas":false,"copyright":false,"https":false,"license":false,"operational":200,"redirects":[{"status":301,"url":"https://sourceforge.net/projects/samtools/"}],"ssl":null,"s